  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nShower and thunderstorm activity will continue to increase through \nthe afternoon and evening hours, most along the Rocky Mountain Front \nand Central Montana mountains, with these showers and storms then \nmoving east to northeast over the adjacent plains and valleys into \nthe morning hours on Thursday. A few storms could produce strong \nwind gusts and/or heavy rainfall this afternoon/evening, with the \nbest opportunity for strong wind gusts occurring across Southwest \nMontana where the sub-cloud layer is still relatively dry leading to \ninverted-V soundings. Additionally, breezy and gusty north to \nnortheast winds will develop over the plains of Central and North \nCentral Montana from the late afternoon hours and persist through \nthe evening hours tonight as the H850 flow increases in conjuction \nwith surface pressure rises on the order of 3-6mb/6hr as surface \nhigh pressure continues to nose south from Canada. - Moldan\n\nThe chance for thunderstorms will increase Thu into Friday morning \nas precipitable water increases. Thus the chance for isolated flash \nflooding is possible from heavier showers. Additionally, a few \nstorms could produce strong wind gusts on Thursday/Friday, \nespecially over Southwest MT where it will be a bit more unstable.\n\nThe summertime upper level ridge of high pressure will try to \nrebuild over the northern Rockies starting this weekend. This will \nresult in warmer, but more unstable air moving back into the CWA \nthis weekend. Expect daily chances for showers/thunderstorms from \nSaturday through Tuesday. Again, the main concern from storms over \nthe weekend will be lightning and strong wind gusts. - Brusda\n\n\n- Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nHeavy Rainfall through Friday night...\n\nEnsemble model guidance continues to support numerous opportunities \nfor showers and thunderstorms through Friday night; with climate \nanomaly indicators suggesting that some of the showers and \nthunderstorms could produce periods of heavy rainfall anomalous to \nAugust climatology, most notably along the Rocky Mountain Front and \nacross portions of Central and Southwest Montana (generally between \nthe I-90 and Montana Hwy 200 corridors). Included below is a table \nlisting the NBM5.0 probabilities for 0.25\", 0.5\" and 1\" for select \nlocations across North Central, Central, and Southwest Montana\n\n                   Rainfall Probabilities thru 6am Saturday\nLOCATION               > 0.25\" |   > 0.50\"   | > 1.00\"\nBrowning                 100%  |      90%    |    65%\nCut Bank                  95%  |      90%    |    50%\nHavre                     95%  |      85%    |    35%\nGreat Falls               90%  |      60%    |    20%\nLewistown                100%  |      85%    |    55%\nHelena                    95%  |      60%    |    25%\nWhite Sulphur Springs    100%  |      90%    |    60%     \nBozeman                   95%  |      80%    |    35%\nDillon                    90%  |      65%    |    25%\nEnnis                     95%  |      80%    |    55%\nWest Yellowstone          95%  |      80%    |    35%\n\nWhile rainfall amounts exceeding 2\" can not be ruled out by Saturday \nmorning, most notably across the Glacier National Park region, most \nareas have a 10% or less chance of seeing this occur. - Moldan&&",
